Use a **union**: one result type whose value is exactly one of three unrelated payload types, with compiler checking that callers handle every case.

I retrieved Playbook’s full `csharp.closed-results` and `json.serialize-closed-results` recipes using `targetVersion: "latest"`. That resolved to catalogue **11.0** for exploration; it does **not** select your application’s target or establish compatibility.

For the conditional **.NET 11 / C# 15 route**, the model could be:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization;

public sealed record SyncCompleted(
    [property: JsonRequired] int AppliedCount);

public sealed record SyncConflict(
    [property: JsonRequired] string ConflictId);

public sealed record SyncUnavailable(
    [property: JsonRequired] string RetryReason);

[JsonUnion(TypeClassifier = typeof(JsonUnionTypeStructuralClassifier))]
public union SyncResult(SyncCompleted, SyncConflict, SyncUnavailable);

[JsonSourceGenerationOptions(GenerationMode = JsonSourceGenerationMode.Metadata)]
[JsonSerializable(typeof(SyncResult))]
internal partial class SyncJson : JsonSerializerContext;
```

Assign a payload directly, then match all three cases:

```csharp
SyncResult result = new SyncCompleted(12);

string description = result switch
{
    SyncCompleted x   => $"Applied {x.AppliedCount}",
    SyncConflict x    => $"Conflict: {x.ConflictId}",
    SyncUnavailable x => $"Unavailable: {x.RetryReason}",
    null              => throw new InvalidOperationException("Unassigned result")
};

string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(result, SyncJson.Default.SyncResult);
SyncResult restored = JsonSerializer.Deserialize(json, SyncJson.Default.SyncResult);
```

The union preserves the existing payload types. An omitted case produces an exhaustiveness warning; treating warnings as errors makes that a build failure. Avoid a catch-all arm that hides newly added cases. [C# union reference](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/builtin-types/union)

JSON contains the active payload directly—for example, `{"AppliedCount":12}`—without a discriminator. Because all three cases are objects, the structural classifier uses their distinct required root properties to select the case. [Union JSON serialization](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/serialization/system-text-json/union-types)

The prerequisites and limitations matter:

- **Compatibility:** this Playbook route requires `net11.0`, .NET 11 SDK/runtime/reference assemblies and `LangVersion` `15.0`. Framework `System.Text.Json` supplies the APIs; no extra package, workload or experimental opt-in is required.
- **Classification:** property values and nested content do not distinguish cases. Unmatched or ambiguous JSON throws. Nested unions, polymorphic cases and `ReferenceHandler.Preserve` are unsupported by this classifier. Property-name changes can affect persisted compatibility.
- **Validation:** required-property presence does not validate business rules or guarantee non-null strings. Default union storage has no active case; here JSON `null` round-trips that unassigned state. Decide whether persistence should reject it.
- **Evidence:** Playbook records real RC2 builds and round trips, **not GA verification or Native AOT publishing**. Exact evidence versions are test baselines, not mandatory installation versions.

A separate documented alternative is a handwritten wrapper with three typed constructors and an explicit JSON converter, delegating payload serialization to generated contracts. Playbook demonstrates that approach on .NET 10/C# 14; it requires application-owned case selection and defensive matching rather than compiler-known exhaustiveness.

Once a target is chosen, verify each case’s identity and payload round-trip, null/default handling, and ambiguous/unmatched rejection. Catalogue coverage is incomplete, so these are not exhaustive compatibility options.

